\vspace{0.5cm}

In a class of 50 students, 30 like Mathematics, 25 like Computer Science,
and 10 like both. How many students like at least one of the two subjects?

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

\vspace{0.3cm}
Let $M$ = set of students who like Maths, $C$ = set who like CS.

\textbf{Given:} $|M| = 30$, $|C| = 25$, $|M \cap C| = 10$.

\textbf{Apply the Inclusion-Exclusion Principle:}
\[
    |M \cup C| = |M| + |C| - |M \cap C| = 30 + 25 - 10 = \boxed{45}
\]

Students who like \textbf{neither}: $50 - 45 = 5$.

\vspace{0.2cm}
\textcolor{accentorange}{\textbf{Note:}} We subtract $|M \cap C|$ because students
in both sets get counted \textit{twice} in $|M| + |C|$,
so we correct by subtracting one copy.

\vspace{0.5cm}

Simplify $(A \cup B) \cap (A \cup \overline{B})$.

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

\vspace{0.3cm}
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{|l|l|}
\hline
\rowcolor{primaryblue!20}
\textbf{Expression} & \textbf{Law Applied} \\
\hline
$(A \cup B) \cap (A \cup \overline{B})$ & Given \\
\hline
$A \cup (B \cap \overline{B})$ & Distributive Law \\
\hline
$A \cup \emptyset$ & Complement Law: $B \cap \overline{B} = \emptyset$ \\
\hline
$A$ & Identity Law \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{darkgreen}{\textbf{Result:}}
\[
    \boxed{(A \cup B) \cap (A \cup \overline{B}) = A}
\]

