\vspace{0.5cm}

A relation $R$ on a set $A$ is a \textbf{partial order} (or \textit{partial ordering})
if it is:

\vspace{0.3cm}
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{|c|l|l|}
\hline
\rowcolor{primaryblue!20}
\textbf{Property} & \textbf{Formal Condition} & \textbf{Example with $\leq$} \\
\hline
\textbf{Reflexive}     & $(a,a) \in R \;\forall a \in A$                   & $a \leq a$ \\
\hline
\textbf{Antisymmetric} & $(a,b)\in R \land (b,a)\in R \Rightarrow a=b$     & $a\leq b \land b\leq a \Rightarrow a=b$ \\
\hline
\textbf{Transitive}    & $(a,b)\in R \land (b,c)\in R \Rightarrow (a,c)\in R$ & $a\leq b \land b\leq c \Rightarrow a\leq c$ \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\vspace{0.4cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{\faInfoCircle\ Key Points:}}
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.5em]
    \item \textbf{Memory aid:} Partial Order = \textbf{R}eflexive +
          \textbf{A}ntisymmetric + \textbf{T}ransitive (``RAT'').
    \item Compare with equivalence relation (RST): partial order replaces
          \textit{symmetric} with \textit{antisymmetric}.
    \item A set $A$ together with a partial order $R$ is called a
          \textbf{partially ordered set} or \textbf{poset}, written $(A, R)$.
    \item We often use the symbol $\preceq$ to denote a generic partial order,
          writing $a \preceq b$ instead of $(a,b) \in R$.
    \item Two elements $a$ and $b$ in a poset are \textbf{comparable} if
          $a \preceq b$ or $b \preceq a$; otherwise they are \textbf{incomparable}.
\end{itemize}

\vspace{0.5cm}

Verify that each of the following is a partial order.

\begin{enumerate}[label=(\alph*), leftmargin=2em]
    \item The relation $\leq$ (less than or equal to) on $\mathbb{Z}$.
    \item The relation $\mid$ (divides) on $\mathbb{Z}^+$.
    \item The relation $\subseteq$ (subset) on $\mathcal{P}(A)$ for any set $A$.
\end{enumerate}

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textbf{(a)} $(\mathbb{Z},\, \leq)$:

\textit{Reflexive:} $a \leq a$ for all $a \in \mathbb{Z}$. \textcolor{darkgreen}{\checkmark}

\textit{Antisymmetric:} $a \leq b$ and $b \leq a$ $\Rightarrow$ $a = b$. \textcolor{darkgreen}{\checkmark}

\textit{Transitive:} $a \leq b$ and $b \leq c$ $\Rightarrow$ $a \leq c$. \textcolor{darkgreen}{\checkmark}

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textbf{(b)} $(\mathbb{Z}^+,\, \mid)$:

\textit{Reflexive:} $a \mid a$ since $a = a \cdot 1$. \textcolor{darkgreen}{\checkmark}

\textit{Antisymmetric:} If $a \mid b$ and $b \mid a$ then $b = ka$ and $a = mb$,
giving $a = mka$, so $mk = 1$. Since $m,k \in \mathbb{Z}^+$, we get $m = k = 1$,
thus $a = b$. \textcolor{darkgreen}{\checkmark}

\textit{Transitive:} If $a \mid b$ and $b \mid c$, then $b = ka$ and $c = lb$,
so $c = l(ka) = (lk)a$, giving $a \mid c$. \textcolor{darkgreen}{\checkmark}

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textbf{(c)} $(\mathcal{P}(A),\, \subseteq)$:

\textit{Reflexive:} $A \subseteq A$ for every set. \textcolor{darkgreen}{\checkmark}

\textit{Antisymmetric:} $A \subseteq B$ and $B \subseteq A$ $\Rightarrow$ $A = B$. \textcolor{darkgreen}{\checkmark}

\textit{Transitive:} $A \subseteq B$ and $B \subseteq C$ $\Rightarrow$ $A \subseteq C$. \textcolor{darkgreen}{\checkmark}

\vspace{0.3cm}
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{|l|c|c|c|}
\hline
\rowcolor{primaryblue!20}
\textbf{Relation} & \textbf{Reflexive} & \textbf{Antisymmetric} & \textbf{Transitive} \\
\hline
$(\mathbb{Z},\leq)$ &
\textcolor{darkgreen}{\checkmark} & \textcolor{darkgreen}{\checkmark} & \textcolor{darkgreen}{\checkmark} \\
\hline
$(\mathbb{Z}^+, \mid)$ &
\textcolor{darkgreen}{\checkmark} & \textcolor{darkgreen}{\checkmark} & \textcolor{darkgreen}{\checkmark} \\
\hline
$(\mathcal{P}(A), \subseteq)$ &
\textcolor{darkgreen}{\checkmark} & \textcolor{darkgreen}{\checkmark} & \textcolor{darkgreen}{\checkmark} \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\vspace{0.5cm}

A partial order $(A, \preceq)$ is a \textbf{total order} (or \textit{linear order}
or \textit{chain}) if \textbf{every} pair of elements is comparable:
\[
    \forall a,b \in A,\; a \preceq b \;\text{ or }\; b \preceq a
\]

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{\faInfoCircle\ Key Points:}}
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.5em]
    \item $(\mathbb{Z}, \leq)$ is a \textbf{total order} --- any two integers are comparable.
    \item $(\mathbb{Z}^+, \mid)$ is \textbf{not} a total order --- for example,
          2 and 3 are incomparable (neither $2\mid 3$ nor $3\mid 2$).
    \item $(\mathcal{P}(A), \subseteq)$ is \textbf{not} a total order in general ---
          $\{1\}$ and $\{2\}$ are incomparable subsets.
\end{itemize}

\vspace{0.5cm}

A \textbf{Hasse diagram} is a simplified visual representation of a finite poset
$(A, \preceq)$ obtained from its directed graph by:

\vspace{0.3cm}
\begin{enumerate}[label=\textbf{Rule \arabic*:}, leftmargin=4em]
    \item \textbf{Remove all loops} (reflexivity is understood).
    \item \textbf{Remove all edges implied by transitivity.}
          If $a \preceq b$ and $b \preceq c$, remove the direct edge $a \to c$.
    \item \textbf{Orient edges upward.} Place $b$ above $a$ whenever $a \prec b$
          (proper ordering), then drop all arrowheads.
\end{enumerate}

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{\faInfoCircle\ Reading a Hasse Diagram:}}
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.5em]
    \item $a \preceq b$ iff you can travel from $a$ \textbf{upward} to $b$
          along the edges.
    \item Elements with no element below them are \textbf{minimal elements}.
    \item Elements with no element above them are \textbf{maximal elements}.
\end{itemize}

\vspace{0.5cm}

\vspace{0.2cm}
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{|l|l|l|}
\hline
\rowcolor{primaryblue!20}
\textbf{Element} & \textbf{Definition} & \textbf{Key Fact} \\
\hline
\textbf{Maximal} & No element strictly above it & May not be unique \\
\hline
\textbf{Minimal} & No element strictly below it & May not be unique \\
\hline
\textbf{Greatest} & Above \textit{every} element & Unique if exists \\
\hline
\textbf{Least} & Below \textit{every} element & Unique if exists \\
\hline
\textbf{Upper bound} of $B$ & Above every element of $B \subseteq A$ & May not exist \\
\hline
\textbf{Lower bound} of $B$ & Below every element of $B \subseteq A$ & May not exist \\
\hline
\textbf{LUB / sup} of $B$ & Least among all upper bounds of $B$ & Unique if exists \\
\hline
\textbf{GLB / inf} of $B$ & Greatest among all lower bounds of $B$ & Unique if exists \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\vspace{0.4cm}
\textcolor{accentorange}{\textbf{Critical Distinction:}}
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.5em]
    \item A \textbf{maximal} element has nothing above it \textit{in the poset}.
          A \textbf{greatest} element is above \textit{every other} element.
    \item A poset can have many maximal elements but at most \textbf{one} greatest element.
\end{itemize}

\vspace{0.5cm}

In a poset $(A, \preceq)$, if a greatest element exists, it is \textbf{unique}.
Similarly, if a least element exists, it is unique.

\vspace{0.5cm}

Draw the Hasse diagram for the divisibility partial order on the set
$S = \{1, 2, 3, 4, 6, 12\}$.

